\begin_layout Section*
Problem 5.12
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the setup of 5.1.2 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},\ldots x_{K})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}\equiv(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K-1},z_{1},\ldots,z_{M})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 (let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=1$
\end_inset

 to allow an intercept), assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{zz}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
 Prove that rank 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{xz}^{\prime})=K$
\end_inset

 if and only if at least one 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}$
\end_inset

 in equation (5.1.5) is different from zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}^{*}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},x_{K}^{*})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 be the linear projection of each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}=\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{1}+\cdots+\theta_{M}z_{M}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}^{*}+\mathbf{r}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{rz}^{\prime})=\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{xz}^{\prime})=E(\mathbf{xz}^{*\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}^{*}=\Pi\mathbf{z}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times L$
\end_inset

 matrix whose first 
\begin_inset Formula $K-1$
\end_inset

 rows are the first 
\begin_inset Formula $K-1$
\end_inset

 unit 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times L$
\end_inset

 matrices, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,0,0,\ldots,0),(0,1,0,\ldots,0),\ldots(0,0,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

, and whose last row is 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\ldots,\delta_{K-1},\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{M})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{xz}^{\prime})=\Pi E(\mathbf{zz}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, so that, because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{zz}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\mathbf{xz}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we know that the first 
\begin_inset Formula $K-1$
\end_inset

 rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 are linearly independent.
 However, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}=0\ \forall\ j=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

, then the last row can be written as a linear combination of the first
 
\begin_inset Formula $K-1$
\end_inset

 rows, which means 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(\Pi)=K-1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(\Pi)=K$
\end_inset

 if and only if at least one 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}\neq0$
\end_inset

, which would then, in turn, make the last row linearly independent.
\end_layout

